> monotonic x^3
monotonic x^3:
  strictly increasing on all real x
  proved symbolically: x^3' = 3x^2; its real roots: 0 (multiplicity 2) (exact); f' keeps its sign between roots of odd multiplicity (zeros of f' of even multiplicity are isolated, so it stays strict)
> monotonic x^3 - 3x
monotonic x^3 - 3x:
  not monotonic on all real x
  maximal intervals of monotonicity:
    x in (-inf, -1]: increasing
    x in [-1, 1]: decreasing
    x in [1, inf): increasing
  witness: x = -2 < x = -1 but f(-2) = -2 < f(-1) = 2 (not decreasing)
  witness: x = -1 < x = 1 but f(-1) = 2 > f(1) = -2 (not increasing)
  proved symbolically: x^3 - 3x' = 3x^2 - 3; its real roots: -1, 1 (exact); f' keeps its sign between roots of odd multiplicity
> monotonic x^2 in 0..5
monotonic x^2:
  strictly increasing on [0, 5]
  proved symbolically: x^2' = 2x; its real roots: 0 (exact); f' keeps its sign between roots of odd multiplicity (zeros of f' of even multiplicity are isolated, so it stays strict)
> monotonic sin(x)
monotonic sin(x):
  not monotonic on [-10, 10]
  maximal intervals of monotonicity:
    x in [-10, -7.853981634 (~-5*pi/2)]: decreasing
    x in [-7.853981634 (~-5*pi/2), -4.71238898 (~-3*pi/2)]: increasing
    x in [-4.71238898 (~-3*pi/2), -1.570796327 (~-pi/2)]: decreasing
    x in [-1.570796327 (~-pi/2), 1.570796327 (~pi/2)]: increasing
    x in [1.570796327 (~pi/2), 4.71238898 (~3*pi/2)]: decreasing
    x in [4.71238898 (~3*pi/2), 7.853981634 (~5*pi/2)]: increasing
    x in [7.853981634 (~5*pi/2), 10]: decreasing
  witness: x = -7.853981634 < x = -4.71238898 but f(-7.853981634) = -1 < f(-4.71238898) = 1 (not decreasing)
  witness: x = -10 < x = -7.853981634 but f(-10) = 0.5440211109 > f(-7.853981634) = -1 (not increasing)
  checked numerically on [-10, 10] (x has no range; give it one); turning points refined on f'
  periodic (period 2*pi ≈ 6.283185307): not monotonic on any interval longer than one period
> monotonic 1/x
monotonic 1/x:
  not monotonic on [-10, 10]
  maximal intervals of monotonicity:
    x in [-10, -0.001]: decreasing
    x in [0.001, 10]: decreasing
  witness: x = -0.001 < x = 0.001 but f(-0.001) = -1000 < f(0.001) = 1000 (not decreasing)
  witness: x = -10 < x = -0.001 but f(-10) = -0.1 > f(-0.001) = -1000 (not increasing)
  checked numerically on [-10, 10] (x has no range; give it one); turning points refined on f'
> monotonic floor(x)
monotonic floor(x):
  non-decreasing (increasing, not strictly) on [-10, 10]
  checked numerically on [-10, 10] (x has no range; give it one); turning points refined on f'
> inc(x: u8) = x + 1
> monotonic inc
monotonic inc:
  not monotonic on 0..255
  maximal intervals of monotonicity:
    x in 0..254: increasing
    x in 254..255: decreasing
  witness: x = 0 < x = 1 but f(0) = 1 < f(1) = 2 (not decreasing)
  witness: x = 254 < x = 255 but f(254) = 255 > f(255) = 0 (not increasing)
  note: without wrapping (as int) it is strictly increasing; the machine type wraps: f(254) = 255, f(255) = 0
  proved, exhaustive over all 256 points (forward difference f(x+1) - f(x))
> dbl(x: u8) = x * 2
> monotonic dbl
monotonic dbl:
  not monotonic on 0..255
  maximal intervals of monotonicity:
    x in 0..127: increasing
    x in 127..128: decreasing
    x in 128..255: increasing
  witness: x = 0 < x = 1 but f(0) = 0 < f(1) = 2 (not decreasing)
  witness: x = 127 < x = 128 but f(127) = 254 > f(128) = 0 (not increasing)
  note: without wrapping (as int) it is strictly increasing; the machine type wraps: f(127) = 254, f(128) = 0
  proved, exhaustive over all 256 points (forward difference f(x+1) - f(x))
> period sin(2x)
period of sin(2x):
  fundamental period pi ≈ 3.141592654
  proved symbolically: sin(2x) has period pi; x occurs only inside them, so f(x + p) = f(x).  Smallest: no p/k (2 <= k <= 64) is a period (checked at 997 points)
> period sin(x) + cos(3x)
period of sin(x) + cos(3x):
  fundamental period 2*pi ≈ 6.283185307
  proved symbolically: sin(x) has period 2*pi, cos(3x) has period 2*pi/3; p is their least common multiple; x occurs only inside them, so f(x + p) = f(x).  Smallest: no p/k (2 <= k <= 64) is a period (checked at 997 points)
> period abs(sin(x))
period of abs(sin(x)):
  fundamental period pi ≈ 3.141592654
  sin(x) has period 2*pi; so 2*pi ≈ 6.283185307 is a period (proved symbolically); its 1/2 also is, checked numerically at 997 points (not proved), and no smaller fraction is
> period tan(x/2)
period of tan(x/2):
  fundamental period 2*pi ≈ 6.283185307
  proved symbolically: tan(x/2) has period 2*pi; x occurs only inside them, so f(x + p) = f(x).  Smallest: no p/k (2 <= k <= 64) is a period (checked at 997 points)
> periodic sin(x) + sin(sqrt(2)*x)
periodic sin(x) + sin(1.414213562x):
  no: not periodic
  the subterms' periods 6.283185307 (~2*pi) and 4.442882938 are incommensurate (ratio 0.7071067812 is not a ratio of small integers)
  sin(x) has period 2*pi, sin(1.414213562x) has period 4.442882938; a sum of such terms is almost periodic, not periodic (unless they cancel)
> periodic x*sin(x)
periodic x*sin(x):
  no: not periodic
  x appears outside the periodic subterms (in x*sin(x)), and no period p <= 20 was found numerically
> periodic x^2 + 1
periodic x^2 + 1:
  no: not periodic
  a polynomial of degree 2 is not periodic (proved: f(x) - f(0) is a non-zero polynomial, so it has finitely many zeros)
> period x - floor(x)
period of x - floor(x):
  fundamental period 1
  found numerically (not proved): f(x + p) = f(x) at 997 points of [-10, 10]
> m7(x: u8) = x % 7
> period m7
period of m7:
  fundamental period 7
  proved, exhaustive: f(x + 7) = f(x) for every x with both in 0..255, and no smaller shift works
  the pattern does not continue across the end of the domain
> a3(x: u8) = x & 3
> period a3
period of a3:
  fundamental period 4
  proved, exhaustive: f(x + 4) = f(x) for every x with both in 0..255, and no smaller shift works
  the pattern also continues across the wrap-around of the type (the period divides the domain size)
> s7(x: i8) = x % 7
> period s7
period of s7:
  fundamental period 7 for x >= 0 only (C's % truncates towards zero, so the pattern breaks at 0; mod repeats everywhere)
  proved, exhaustive: f(x + 7) = f(x) for every x >= 0 with both in 0..127, and no smaller shift works
  the pattern does not continue across the end of the domain
> parity x^2*cos(x)
parity of x^2*cos(x):
  even: f(-x) = f(x)
  proved symbolically (parity rules: x is odd; sums keep a common parity, products multiply it; sin/tan/atan preserve it, cos/abs make it even)
> parity x^3 + sin(x)
parity of x^3 + sin(x):
  odd: f(-x) = -f(x)
  proved symbolically (parity rules: x is odd; sums keep a common parity, products multiply it; sin/tan/atan preserve it, cos/abs make it even)
> parity x^2 + x
parity of x^2 + x:
  neither even nor odd
  not even: f(x) = 2, f(-x) = 0 at x = 1
  not odd: f(x) = 2, f(-x) = 0 at x = 1
  checked numerically at 2000 points of [-10, 10]
> sq8(x: i8) = x * x
> parity sq8
parity of sq8:
  even: f(-x) = f(x)
  proved, exhaustive, with -x and -f(x) in i8 arithmetic (two's complement: -x of the minimum wraps to itself)
> parity dbl
parity of dbl:
  odd: f(-x) = -f(x)
  proved, exhaustive, with -x and -f(x) in u8 arithmetic (two's complement: -x is 2^w - x)
