> injective x^2
injective x^2:
  injective: no
  witness: f(-1) = f(1) = 1 with x = -1 and x = 1
  the value is taken on two pieces of monotonicity (pieces proved, witness solved by bisection)
> injective x^3
injective x^3:
  injective: yes (one-to-one)
  x^3 is strictly increasing, so no two inputs share a value (proved)
> injective 1/x
injective 1/x:
  injective: yes (one-to-one)
  strictly monotonic on each piece of its domain with disjoint value ranges (checked numerically)
> surjective x^2
surjective x^2:
  surjective onto the reals: no
  witness: -1 is never taken
  f is bounded below by 0: proved: an even-degree polynomial with a positive leading coefficient has a minimum, 0 at x = 0 (a root of f')
> surjective exp(x)
surjective exp(x):
  surjective onto the reals: no
  witness: -1 is never taken
  f is bounded below by 4.539992976e-05: proved by interval arithmetic: f >= 0; the smallest value seen is 4.539992976e-05 at x = -10
> surjective x^2 onto 0..4
surjective x^2:
  surjective onto [0, 4]: yes
  continuous on one interval, with values from 0 to inf, so every value between is taken (intermediate value theorem; continuity proved only for elementary bodies, the extremes checked numerically)
> bijective x^3 + x
bijective x^3 + x:
  injective: yes (one-to-one)
  x^3 + x is strictly increasing, so no two inputs share a value (proved)
  surjective onto the reals: yes
  proved: a polynomial of odd degree 3 is continuous and tends to -inf and +inf, so it takes every value (intermediate value theorem)
  bijective: yes
